#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175762 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/id #uuid "53bf6a59-06ab-5597-999c-20f7ca55bba2" :question/math-academy-id "e-9088" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $\\dfrac{a}{b}$." :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4\n$$\n\nAs a result, the first column of $P$ corresponds to an eigenvector of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$.\n\nNow, computing $A-4I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 4I &= \\begin{bmatrix}8 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} - (4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 \\\\ -4 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$, which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -4 & 0\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain $x_1=-x_2$. Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=1$, we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{a}{b}=\\dfrac{-1}{1} = -1\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175763 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/id #uuid "90e87dca-f423-5587-b39a-5d6e931db18e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9089" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the second column of the matrix $P$." :question/worked-solution "From the Jordan matrix $J$, we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=4\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that\n\n- the first column of $P$ corresponds to the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ of the eigenvalue $\\lambda=4$,\n- the second column of $P$ corresponds to the vector $\\mathbf{v}_2$ such that $(A-4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1$.\n\nNow, computing $A-4I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 4I &= \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - (4)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 \\\\ -5 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix equation\n\n$$\n(A - 4I)\\mathbf{v}_2 = \\mathbf{v}_1\n$$\n\nis equivalent to the system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nM &= \\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ -5 & -5 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{2} \\mid:=R_{2} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}5 & 5 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix above has one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, $x_2$ is a free variable. From the first equation, we obtain\n\n$$\nx_1=-\\dfrac 15 - x_2\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{5} - x_{2} \\\\ x_{2}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTherefore, setting $x_2=0$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ b\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175785 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/id #uuid "b4a6d165-9c52-59e6-af4b-c5777066e877" :question/math-academy-id "e-9227" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = P J P^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the second column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that the Jordan matrix $J$ consists of one Jordan block $J = J_2(1)$.\n\nTherefore, the columns of $P$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\nJ_{2}(1) \\mid: \\mid v_{2}→^(A - 1 I)v_{1}→^(A - 1 I)0\n$$\n\nAs a result, the second column of $P$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$ corresponding to $\\lambda=1$.\n\nNow, computing $A-1I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 1I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix} - 1\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAmong the given vectors, only $\\mathbf x$ is a generalized eigenvector of rank $2$, since\n\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\nand\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}→^(A - 1 I)\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}→^(A - 1 I)0\n$$\n\nThe other vectors do not satisfy this condition."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ce145732-4daa-5c68-bec0-1e7ba678cec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173551} {:answer/id #uuid "30b95d73-cd39-51b6-9de2-5af01b1c7e04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173552} {:answer/id #uuid "3b6a0bf5-cf26-5b45-857d-aba7a4799609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173553} {:answer/id #uuid "4c06d87b-96a2-5f68-a7a1-243ae1f9a5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173554} {:answer/id #uuid "5fd835e4-ef02-51c1-83f4-566583bb909b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173555}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c06d87b-96a2-5f68-a7a1-243ae1f9a5e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173554} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d153c99-a625-5397-b0e6-129ef4af5298" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173550}] :question/id #uuid "b0fdb675-21a9-5763-9fe7-cb59859241e7" :question/math-academy-id "q-108728" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $a - 2b$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176156 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e22ff208-ff10-5106-8922-26adc9007e40" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The First Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7b3a9e2-58e8-5498-b3ad-3245b717574f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173599} {:answer/id #uuid "0c3c84ac-e05e-53fb-91e4-46f3a27e6997" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173600} {:answer/id #uuid "3eb202cb-9a10-553f-8be3-a81f2e4cbd45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173601} {:answer/id #uuid "5d8eb27b-da2a-569a-9358-53ea0291922b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173602} {:answer/id #uuid "6d540e91-3815-51d8-87f0-02a314bf2df1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b3a9e2-58e8-5498-b3ad-3245b717574f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173599} :answer-field/id #uuid "195417ba-da51-500f-88d3-50d8726a4ddc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173598}] :question/id #uuid "addb688e-b364-54ad-8e80-207a23ed2756" :question/math-academy-id "q-109368" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}9 & 4 \\\\ -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}a & ∗ \\\\ b & ∗\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $\\frac{b}{a}$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176157 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac67bb72-7802-547d-b9d9-ab238cc3768f" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Elements of a Jordan Canonical Decomposition for a 2x2 Matrix: The Second Column Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a79a0082-1dff-588d-bd51-b7881690182a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173605} {:answer/id #uuid "db4da122-d18d-57f4-af85-19d8adfa4b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173606} {:answer/id #uuid "dd840fc7-d8ad-5a86-b676-ed88f9534b30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173607} {:answer/id #uuid "e63a0c7b-91d2-5beb-8fb6-3939ac0e4efd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173608} {:answer/id #uuid "645af67c-6061-5b15-bdc4-67c1efe66121" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db4da122-d18d-57f4-af85-19d8adfa4b83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173606} :answer-field/id #uuid "8470e3e6-af6c-5e75-9875-64214dc459e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173604}] :question/id #uuid "71955644-7fce-58e7-aa23-c8e43615d89a" :question/math-academy-id "q-109381" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, P = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & a \\\\ 1 & b\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, find the value of $a - 2b$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176202 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53e40a15-120c-5587-979b-cc1369d244bf" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Columns of a Transformation Matrix in a Jordan Decomposition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "862dd746-e765-5276-9c3d-a3df22502c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173875} {:answer/id #uuid "96dc658c-f069-51f0-ab2d-d1edef81492e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173876} {:answer/id #uuid "a76729e7-40ac-5002-883b-ca750e2e549d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173877} {:answer/id #uuid "d95e6af2-6a09-532e-b47a-027634bf7fca" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ and $z$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173878} {:answer/id #uuid "5d4fcc32-97fa-52dd-a9a0-5800b1d12609" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$y$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "862dd746-e765-5276-9c3d-a3df22502c34" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173875} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b89bfc-b72f-5819-96e0-040551b0f540" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186173874}] :question/id #uuid "7e0868ec-2423-5a95-9212-2ae581f51f78" :question/math-academy-id "q-112432" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}, J = \\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nConsider the matrices above. Given that $A = PJP^{-1}$ is a Jordan canonical decomposition of $A$, which of the following could be the first column of the matrix $P$?\n\n$x = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}, y = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}, z = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$"}]}
